  "image_name": "f00e8e4ac01b9c87c2ec6ca2dac69ffa.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how molecular building blocks assemble into larger soft materials and how stimuli can control that assembly. It groups the driving forces into supramolecular interactions, chemical reactions, and interparticle interactions. In the first group, hydrophobic effects, hydrogen bonding, π–π stacking, electrostatic attraction, and coordination bonding are shown converting small molecules or chains into organized structures such as entangled fibers, rings, compact clusters, core–shell-like aggregates, and star-shaped assemblies. Chemical routes, including condensation and radical polymerization, similarly link monomers into crosslinked networks or particulate aggregates. A dipole–dipole example illustrates particles aligning and packing into an ordered array.\n\nA second section lists triggers that can regulate these processes. Endogenous stimuli include pH, redox reactions, and enzymes, while exogenous stimuli include light, magnetic fields, and ultrasound.\n\nThe final schematic presents an energy landscape for assembly. Monomers start at higher energy, can be pushed by a stimulus into a metastable assembled state, and then, by overcoming an energy barrier, relax into a lower-energy thermodynamically stable state. Overall, the figure conveys that different interaction types and external cues guide nanoscale self-assembly and determine whether products are transient or stable.",
